\name{cellucid_vector_field_summary}
\alias{cellucid_vector_field_summary}
\title{Summarize where cells are heading}
\usage{
cellucid_vector_field_summary(
  viewer,
  by,
  categories,
  neighbors = 30,
  vector_field,
  view,
  visible_only = FALSE
)
}
\arguments{
\item{viewer}{A viewer \code{\link{cellucid_view}()} or \code{\link{cellucid_serve}()} returned.}
\item{by}{The categorical field to group the cells by. A named list. Required. \describe{\item{\code{key}}{The column name as the app shows it. Text (at least 1 character). Required.}\item{\code{source}}{Exactly \code{"obs"}. Required.}}}
\item{categories}{Only these categories of \code{by} (else every category with cells); their arrows still lead into every category. A vector or list of text (at least 1 character) (at least 1 item, each once). Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{neighbors}{How many nearest cells (in the embedding) each cell's arrow is shared among. A whole number (at least 2). Default \code{30}, the viewer's own.}
\item{vector_field}{The vector field, by name (dataset_summary lists them). Left out: the one the overlay shows, else the dataset's default, else the only one bound to the view's embedding; with several bound and none of those, name one. Text (at least 1 character). Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{view}{The view whose embedding the arrows are read in: "View N"; the selected view when left out. Text. Left out unless given (the viewer then chooses as its own control does).}
\item{visible_only}{Only the cells the view shows (its filters and hidden categories), as cells and as neighbors. \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}. Default \code{FALSE}, the viewer's own.}
}
\value{
A \code{cellucid_action_request}, at once and invisibly: the action is sent now, or when the viewer's channel opens.
\code{\link{cellucid_action_result}(request)} returns what it read: a list of \code{by}, \code{cells} (Cells read (with a vector and a category)), \code{embedding}, \code{empty} (Categories left out for having no cells read), \code{groups}, \code{neighbors}, \code{vector_field}, \code{view}, \code{without_vector} (Cells left out for having no vector). Cells in it are original positions (0-based rows of the dataset's obs).
}
\description{
Where the cells of each category of a field are heading along a vector field (RNA velocity, a drift), in the embedding a view shows: per category, how fast (mean speed relative to the mean cell), how aligned its arrows are, their mean direction in the view's axes, and the categories its arrows lead into (each cell's arrow shared among its nearest cells by how directly it points at them, as scVelo's velocity graph does, here in the embedding). Read from the arrows as drawn in that embedding, not from the genes: an embedding can bend, merge or reverse a flow.
}
\details{
The viewer's action \code{vector-field.summary} (Dataset). It reads, and changes nothing: it makes no step of the history.
}
\seealso{
\code{\link{cellucid_act}()} sends any action by its type, and \code{\link{cellucid_action_result}()}
collects its answer.
}
\examples{
if (interactive() && requireNamespace("SeuratObject", quietly = TRUE)) {
  data("pbmc_small", package = "SeuratObject")
  viewer <- cellucid_view(SeuratObject::UpdateSeuratObject(pbmc_small))
  request <- cellucid_vector_field_summary(viewer, by = list(key = "cell_type",
      source = "obs"))
  print(request)
  # cellucid_action_result(request) waits for what it read.
  cellucid_stop(viewer)
}
}
